    "slug": "philippines-holiday-service-calendar",
    "title": "Map Philippine holidays into a client service calendar",
    "excerpt": "A practical way to align Philippine public holidays with client deadlines, coverage decisions, and payroll cutoffs before the quarter starts.",
    "minutes": 6,
    "publicationDate": "2026-11-04",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-04",
    "image": "/daily04/outsourcedlabor/philippines-holiday-service-calendar.png",
    "body": [
      "## Start with the official holiday list",
      "A service calendar is only as reliable as the holiday list behind it, so begin with the official proclamations rather than a remembered list from last year. The Philippine national government publishes regular holidays and special non-working days separately, and the two categories carry different coverage expectations. Ask the provider to attach the source document it used for each date. A calendar that cannot show its source is a guess with a deadline attached.",
      "Check the proclamation that applies to the current year and confirm whether any date has been amended. Special non-working days are sometimes declared close to the date, especially around local fiestas or national observances. Keep the source link and the date you checked it beside the calendar entry. A reviewer should be able to see whether the team used an official list or a commercial calendar that may lag behind.",
      "## Separate national holidays from local observances",
      "A worker based in Metro Manila and a worker based in Cebu do not necessarily observe the same local holidays. National proclamations apply across the country, while city or provincial fiestas are set locally and may still affect attendance. Record the worker's actual work location and the local observance that applies there. Do not assume that a single national calendar covers a distributed team.",
      "Local observances matter most when the worker handles customer-facing coverage or a payroll cutoff that falls on the same day. Ask the provider to list the local dates that affect each assigned worker, not the whole company. If a local date is uncertain, mark it as pending rather than guessing. The calendar should show both the national layer and the local layer so a reviewer can see which one created the conflict.",
      "## Mark the client's own non-working days",
      "The client's holiday schedule may not match the provider's, and that gap is where coverage failures usually start. Add the client's observed holidays, fiscal calendar, and customer promise dates to the same view. A day that is a normal working day in the Philippines may be a closed office for the client. The calendar should make that mismatch visible before the quarter begins.",
      "Include the client's peak periods as well as its closures, because a short week can concentrate more work into fewer days. Mark the dates when a deliverable is due, not only the dates when the office is closed. If the client observes a holiday that the provider does not, decide whether the provider works through it or shifts the deadline. Record that decision with the name of the person who approved it.",
      "## Name the coverage decision for each overlap",
      "For every overlap between a provider holiday and a client deadline, name the coverage decision instead of leaving it to the week of the event. The options are usually to work the holiday with a premium, shift the deadline, split the work across a backup, or accept a reduced service level. Each option has a different cost and a different risk, so the client owner should choose rather than the assistant. Write the choice beside the date so the next planner can see why the calendar looks the way it does.",
      "Record the decision, the approving manager, and the date it takes effect. If the decision is to work the holiday, state who is authorized to approve the premium and how the hours will be verified. If the decision is to shift the deadline, tell the client in writing and confirm the new date. A coverage decision that exists only in a chat message will not survive the first payroll review. Write the decision in the calendar entry so the next reviewer sees it beside the date.",
      "## Move payroll and reporting cutoffs deliberately",
      "A holiday can move a payroll cutoff without moving the payroll system's deadline, which is how late inputs become missing inputs. Identify every cutoff that falls within two working days of a holiday and decide whether to move the internal deadline earlier. Tell the payroll team and the approvers about the changed date at the same time. Do not let the change reach the payroll system through an informal message.",
      "The same logic applies to client reports, invoice runs, and quality reviews. Move the internal preparation date, not the external commitment, unless the client has agreed to a new date. Record the old date, the new date, and the reason in the calendar entry. After the period closes, check whether the earlier cutoff actually prevented a late input.",
      "## Tell the client what changes and what does not",
      "A holiday notice should say which dates are affected, which deliverables move, and which services continue unchanged. Keep the notice short enough to read and specific enough to act on. Avoid a general statement that the team will be unavailable, because it leaves the client guessing about every open item. Name the contact who will handle urgent issues during the closure.",
      "Send the notice early enough for the client to adjust its own commitments, and repeat it in the weekly status note as the date approaches. If the client asks for coverage that was not planned, route the request to the account owner rather than promising it in the reply. Confirm any change in writing so both sides work from the same calendar. Keep the client's acknowledgement with the coverage decision.",
      "## Keep a live holiday register",
      "A holiday register is a small table with the date, the jurisdiction, the source, the coverage decision, and the owner. Keep it in one place that both the client and the provider can read, rather than in a document that only one team updates. Add a column for the date the entry was last checked. The register should be short enough to review in a few minutes each quarter.",
      "When a new proclamation appears, add the date and mark the affected coverage decisions for review. Do not overwrite the earlier entry, because the history shows why a deadline moved. If a date is later cancelled or changed, keep the original and add the correction. A register with a visible history is easier to audit than a calendar that silently changes.",
      "## Review the calendar at the start of each quarter",
      "Review the next quarter's calendar before the first payroll cutoff, not after the first missed deadline. Check the official list, the client's calendar, and the local observances for each worker. Confirm that every overlap has a named coverage decision and an owner. Then send the summary to the client and the payroll team.",
      "At the review, ask one question about the previous quarter: which holiday caused an unplanned change, and what would have prevented it? Record the answer as a change to the register or the cutoff rule. Close the review with one owner and one date for the next check. The goal is a calendar the team can trust without rebuilding it every month."
    ]
